<h2 id="3-let-setup-finish-then-ask-a-question">3. Let setup finish, then ask a question<a class="headerlink" href="#3-let-setup-finish-then-ask-a-question" title="Permanent link">&para;</a></h2>
<p>Wait until Muse reports the storage folder, checksum result, and number of
indexed files. Then ask in the same side chat:</p>
<pre><code class="language-text">How do I use tbl.where? Give me a hint and cite the lecture or textbook
source you used.
</code></pre>
<p>A useful response should explain the concept, cite course material, and avoid
providing assignment solution code.</p>
<h2 id="data-and-privacy-boundaries">Data and privacy boundaries<a class="headerlink" href="#data-and-privacy-boundaries" title="Permanent link">&para;</a></h2>
<p>Muse stores the corpus on a dedicated Secure VM. Meta's
<a href="https://about.fb.com/news/2026/09/introducing-muse-personal-ai-agent/">product announcement</a>
says that computer is contained so another person's agent cannot reach it,
and that conversations and VM data are not shared with Meta's ad systems.
People can opt out of having interactions used to train Meta's AI models.
Review Muse's current privacy and permission settings before pasting the
tutor.</p>
<p>Side chats organize topics, but Muse remains aware across the main chat and
side chats. Do not treat a side chat as a security boundary.</p>
<div class="admonition warning">
<p class="admonition-title">Course materials include restricted solution files</p>
<p>The Spring 2026 corpus includes solution and answer-key files. The pasted
tutor instructs Muse not to disclose them, but prompt instructions are
not a technical access control. Do not share the Muse account with
students.</p>
</div>
<p><a href="https://www.meta.com/help/artificial-intelligence/1331373868832401/">Muse can be inaccurate or take unexpected actions</a>.
Watch first-use setup, and keep write access to connected apps as narrow as
you need.</p>
